<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Spectrune installer — built with wixl (msitools), the Linux-hosted
  WiX-compatible MSI compiler, same cross-build story as the Go binary
  itself (GOOS=windows from this same Linux machine). Output is a real
  Windows .msi; nothing here runs anywhere but Windows.

  Deliberately NOT the AmneziaWG client's own installer pattern (a
  compiled customactions.dll driving ServiceInstall/split-tunnel-driver
  trust/etc.) — Spectrune only needs two things after files land on disk:
  register+start its own Windows service, and drop a desktop shortcut.
  The exe already knows how to register itself (/installservice,
  service.go's installService — same CreateService call any installer
  would make), so custom actions just shell out to the freshly-installed
  exe instead of duplicating that logic in MSI-native tables.

  Everything renamed to Spectrune throughout — install folder, exe name,
  service name, ProgramData folder (see service.go/profiles.go) — this
  install replaces the earlier "aspt-bridge" naming entirely, including
  on-disk paths, not just user-facing text.

  Releasing an UPDATE (not a fresh install): edit the two <?define?>
  values right below — ProductVersion (bump it) and ProductCode (a fresh
  GUID) — then rebuild with the plain command below. UpgradeCode on the
  Product element must never change (it's what MajorUpgrade uses to
  recognize "this is the same product, just a newer version" and silently
  uninstall-then-install in place — no manual removal needed first).
  Component Guids (SpectruneExeComponent, DesktopShortcutComponent) must
  NOT change either — they identify "the same resource" release over
  release. wixl's -D command-line overrides don't win over an in-file
  <?define?> of the same name here (tested — the first definition wins,
  unlike real WiX's candle.exe), so editing these two lines directly is
  the reliable path, not passing -D on the command line.

  Windows Installer only compares the first three Version fields
  (Major.Minor.Build) for upgrade purposes — 1.0.0.0 -> 1.0.0.1 alone
  would NOT be seen as newer, so bump at least the third field.
  ProgramData (profiles, service.log) lives outside the MSI's file
  tracking entirely, so it survives an upgrade untouched either way.

  MUST build with: wixl -ext ui -a x64 spectrune.wxs -o spectrune.msi
  (the ui extension flag and the x64 arch flag both matter). The x64
  arch flag is not optional. Without it, ProgramFiles64Folder
  resolves to "Program Files (x86)" instead of "Program Files" on this
  64-bit target even though Win64="yes" is set on every Component;
  that attribute only marks components as 64-bit in the MSI's own
  tables, it does NOT steer which physical folder ProgramFiles64Folder
  maps to — only the package's target arch does. Confirmed live
  2026-09-06: a rebuild without -a x64 silently installed to the wrong
  folder and repointed the already-running service's registration at
  a path that doesn't match where the old install's files actually
  were, breaking the service until manually uninstalled and
  reinstalled correctly.
-->
<Wix xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/wix/2006/wi">
  <?define ProductCode = "483B79E2-5EC0-40B6-BEAB-4B49E1C659CA" ?>
  <?define ProductVersion = "1.9.3.0" ?>
  <!--
    Name includes the version deliberately — [ProductName] is what every
    bundled wizard dialog's title bar and "Welcome to the ... Setup
    Wizard"/"Ready to install ..." text actually substitutes (those
    dialogs come from wixl's read-only ui extension, so their text can't
    be edited directly); putting the version here is what makes it show
    up everywhere in the wizard without forking those files. Also shows
    up as the Add/Remove Programs display name — normal for installers
    (browsers etc. do the same).
  -->
  <Product Id="$(var.ProductCode)"
           Name="Spectrune $(var.ProductVersion)"
           Language="1033"
           Version="$(var.ProductVersion)"
           Manufacturer="Spectrune"
           UpgradeCode="19478094-F9F8-42A8-8950-08CE0458D57B">

    <Package Id="*"
             InstallerVersion="500"
             Compressed="yes"
             InstallScope="perMachine"
             Description="Spectrune — per-application VPN routing" />

    <!--
      Hand-rolled MajorUpgrade equivalent, not the <MajorUpgrade> shorthand
      — its Schedule attribute isn't recognized by this wixl version
      (silently ignored, logged as a GLib-CRITICAL at build time), so
      RemoveExistingProducts always lands at its default early slot
      (right after InstallValidate, before InstallInitialize). Reproduced
      live 2026-09-03: at that early position, RemoveExistingProducts runs
      the OLD product's own UninstallService deferred custom action before
      a valid script context exists for it, and Windows Installer aborts
      the whole install with error 2762 ("the action must be scheduled
      between InstallInitialize and InstallFinalize"). Also reproduced:
      RemoveExistingProducts refuses any arbitrary sequence position at
      all — error 2613 ("sequenced incorrectly") — it can ONLY sit right
      after one of a few Microsoft-blessed anchors (InstallValidate,
      InstallInitialize, InstallExecute, InstallExecuteAgain), so
      After="InstallFiles" alone isn't valid either; it has to be
      After="InstallExecute" specifically (the "afterInstallExecute"
      schedule <MajorUpgrade> would normally offer, added explicitly
      below since wixl won't take it via that shorthand). This runs the
      old product's removal once the new files are already staged, inside
      a properly bracketed script — safe here specifically because
      SpectruneExeComponent/DesktopShortcutComponent's Guids never change
      release to release (see the release-process comment above): MSI
      recognizes the old and new product as sharing the same components
      by that Guid and re-points ownership rather than deleting the files
      the new product just installed.
    -->
    <Upgrade Id="19478094-F9F8-42A8-8950-08CE0458D57B">
      <UpgradeVersion Minimum="0.0.0.0" IncludeMinimum="yes"
                       Maximum="$(var.ProductVersion)" IncludeMaximum="no"
                       Property="WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED" OnlyDetect="no" />
      <UpgradeVersion Minimum="$(var.ProductVersion)" IncludeMinimum="no"
                       OnlyDetect="yes" Property="WIX_DOWNGRADE_DETECTED" />
    </Upgrade>
    <!-- wixl auto-populates SecureCustomProperties from the Upgrade
         element's Property references above — declaring it again here
         collides with that (duplicate Property-table primary key) and
         silently aborts the build ("libmsi_query_execute", no other
         diagnostic — found by bisecting a minimal repro). -->
    <Condition Message="A newer version of Spectrune is already installed.">
      NOT WIX_DOWNGRADE_DETECTED OR Installed
    </Condition>
    <Media Id="1" Cabinet="spectrune.cab" EmbedCab="yes" />

    <Icon Id="AppIcon" SourceFile="../assets/app.ico" />
    <Property Id="ARPPRODUCTICON" Value="AppIcon" />
    <Property Id="ARPNOMODIFY" Value="1" />

    <Directory Id="TARGETDIR" Name="SourceDir">
      <Directory Id="ProgramFiles64Folder">
        <Directory Id="INSTALLFOLDER" Name="Spectrune">
          <Component Id="SpectruneExeComponent" Guid="CD9AB35D-F615-4D22-ABA6-6A413A6F5041" Win64="yes">
            <File Id="SpectruneExe" Name="spectrune.exe" Source="../spectrune.exe" KeyPath="yes" />
            <!-- The Wintun adapter driver DLL — loaded at runtime via
                 LoadLibrary from the exe's own directory
                 (golang.zx2c4.com/wintun), so it has to ship right next
                 to spectrune.exe. Missed on the first installer pass:
                 every manual deploy this session copied it once early on
                 and never touched it again, so its absence from the MSI
                 went unnoticed until a real from-scratch install. -->
            <File Id="WintunDll" Name="wintun.dll" Source="../wintun.dll" />
          </Component>
        </Directory>
      </Directory>

      <Directory Id="DesktopFolder" Name="Desktop">
        <Component Id="DesktopShortcutComponent" Guid="F014359E-2E63-42E7-B70D-E92C4411A7FB" Win64="yes">
          <Shortcut Id="SpectruneDesktopShortcut"
                    Name="Spectrune"
                    Description="Per-application VPN routing"
                    Target="[#SpectruneExe]"
                    Arguments="/gui"
                    WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                    Icon="AppIcon" />
          <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                          Key="Software\Spectrune"
                          Name="DesktopShortcutInstalled"
                          Type="integer"
                          Value="1"
                          KeyPath="yes" />
        </Component>
      </Directory>

      <!-- Start Menu entry, added 2026-09-06 — before this, Spectrune
           only showed up via its Desktop shortcut, not in the Start Menu
           search/list at all. Own Guid (never to change release to
           release, same rule as the other two components here), own
           subfolder so it groups under "Spectrune" instead of dumping a
           bare shortcut into the root Programs list. -->
      <Directory Id="ProgramMenuFolder">
        <Directory Id="SpectruneProgramMenuFolder" Name="Spectrune">
          <Component Id="StartMenuShortcutComponent" Guid="7CB70FCE-33F3-4243-999E-03BC978F9751" Win64="yes">
            <Shortcut Id="SpectruneStartMenuShortcut"
                      Name="Spectrune"
                      Description="Per-application VPN routing"
                      Target="[#SpectruneExe]"
                      Arguments="/gui"
                      WorkingDirectory="INSTALLFOLDER"
                      Icon="AppIcon" />
            <RemoveFolder Id="RemoveSpectruneProgramMenuFolder" On="uninstall" />
            <RegistryValue Root="HKLM"
                            Key="Software\Spectrune"
                            Name="StartMenuShortcutInstalled"
                            Type="integer"
                            Value="1"
                            KeyPath="yes" />
          </Component>
        </Directory>
      </Directory>
    </Directory>

    <Feature Id="MainFeature" Title="Spectrune" Level="1">
      <ComponentRef Id="SpectruneExeComponent" />
      <ComponentRef Id="DesktopShortcutComponent" />
      <ComponentRef Id="StartMenuShortcutComponent" />
    </Feature>

    <!--
      Register the service right after its exe lands on disk (fresh
      install or upgrade) — skipped on a plain uninstall, where REMOVE is
      set and there's no point registering something about to be deleted.
      Runs elevated/deferred since the MSI itself already required
      elevation to write to Program Files and HKLM.
    -->
    <CustomAction Id="InstallService"
                  FileKey="SpectruneExe"
                  ExeCommand="/installservice"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="check" />

    <!--
      Unregister the service before its exe gets deleted — has to run
      while the file still exists, hence Before="RemoveFiles". Only on a
      real uninstall (REMOVE="ALL"), not on an upgrade's remove-old-files
      pass, so a version bump doesn't drop and re-add the service.
    -->
    <CustomAction Id="UninstallService"
                  FileKey="SpectruneExe"
                  ExeCommand="/uninstallservice"
                  Execute="deferred"
                  Impersonate="no"
                  Return="ignore" />

    <!--
      Explicit numeric Sequence values on both Custom actions below, not
      After/Before — isolated and confirmed a wixl sequencer bug: with two
      Custom actions present where one of them chains via After off a
      LATE standard action (RemoveExistingProducts, itself only
      resolvable after InstallExecute's fixed slot), wixl silently
      re-resolves EVERY Custom action's position off that same late
      anchor, regardless of its own individual After/Before hint —
      UninstallService kept landing at sequence 6503 (after RemoveFiles,
      i.e. after its own target file was already deleted) no matter what
      hint or document position it was given, as long as InstallService's
      After="RemoveExistingProducts" entry was also present. A single
      Custom action alone always resolved its hint correctly; explicit
      Sequence numbers sidestep whatever's misfiring in the two-action
      case entirely. 1801 = right after UnpublishFeatures (1800), well
      before RemoveFiles (3500). 6502 = right after RemoveExistingProducts
      (6501, itself right after InstallExecute's fixed 6500 slot), before
      InstallFinalize (6600). Re-verify both numbers with
      `msiinfo export spectrune.msi InstallExecuteSequence` after any
      change near this block — they're only valid because nothing else in
      the standard sequence sits between UnpublishFeatures/RemoveFiles or
      InstallExecute/InstallFinalize right now.
    -->
    <InstallExecuteSequence>
      <FindRelatedProducts After="AppSearch" />
      <Custom Action="UninstallService" Sequence="1801">REMOVE="ALL"</Custom>
      <!-- Must run before InstallService below: the old product's own
           uninstall (driven from here) stops+deletes a service with the
           exact same name ("SpectruneService" never changes release to
           release) — if InstallService ran first, this would immediately
           delete the service the new product just registered. -->
      <InstallExecute After="InstallFiles" />
      <RemoveExistingProducts After="InstallExecute" />
      <Custom Action="InstallService" Sequence="6502">NOT REMOVE</Custom>
    </InstallExecuteSequence>

    <UIRef Id="SpectruneUI" />
    <UIRef Id="WixUI_Common" />
  </Product>

  <!--
    Custom install-location wizard page — wixl's bundled "ui" extension
    only ships WixUI_Minimal (Welcome -> EULA -> Verify -> Progress ->
    Exit, no directory step) and has no BrowseDlg, so a full graphical
    folder-tree browser isn't available here. This uses the standard MSI
    PathEdit control instead: a plain editable text field bound to
    INSTALLFOLDER, pre-filled with the default path — the user can type a
    different drive/folder (e.g. "D:\Spectrune") directly. Everything else
    (WelcomeDlg, VerifyReadyDlg, ProgressDlg, ExitDialog, and their own
    dependency dialogs like CancelDlg/ErrorDlg) comes from the bundled
    "ui" extension (wixl's "ext ui" flag); only the Next/Back navigation
    between them is wired here, since none of those dialogs hardcode a
    next-dialog target for the plain (non-EULA, non-patch) install path.

    In its own Fragment (this wixl version rejects a bare UI element
    directly under Product) with an explicit Id so Product can pull it in
    via UIRef, same pattern the bundled WixUI_Minimal.wxs itself uses.
  -->
  <Fragment>
    <UI Id="SpectruneUI">
      <TextStyle Id="WixUI_Font_Normal" FaceName="Tahoma" Size="8" />
      <TextStyle Id="WixUI_Font_Bigger" FaceName="Tahoma" Size="12" />
      <TextStyle Id="WixUI_Font_Title" FaceName="Tahoma" Size="9" Bold="yes" />
      <Property Id="DefaultUIFont" Value="WixUI_Font_Normal" />

      <DialogRef Id="CancelDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="ErrorDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="ExitDialog" />
      <DialogRef Id="FatalError" />
      <DialogRef Id="FilesInUse" />
      <DialogRef Id="MsiRMFilesInUse" />
      <DialogRef Id="OutOfDiskDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="OutOfRbDiskDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="PrepareDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="ProgressDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="ResumeDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="UserExit" />
      <DialogRef Id="VerifyReadyDlg" />
      <DialogRef Id="WelcomeDlg" />

      <Dialog Id="InstallDirDlg" Width="370" Height="270" Title="[ProductName] Setup">
        <Control Id="BannerBitmap" Type="Bitmap" X="0" Y="0" Width="370" Height="44" TabSkip="no" Text="WixUI_Bmp_Banner" />
        <Control Id="BannerLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="44" Width="370" Height="0" />
        <Control Id="BottomLine" Type="Line" X="0" Y="234" Width="370" Height="0" />

        <Control Id="Title" Type="Text" X="20" Y="15" Width="300" Height="15" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="{\WixUI_Font_Title}Choose Install Location" />
        <Control Id="Description" Type="Text" X="20" Y="30" Width="330" Height="12" Transparent="yes" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Choose the folder in which to install [ProductName]." />
        <Control Id="FolderLabel" Type="Text" X="20" Y="63" Width="330" Height="24" NoPrefix="yes" Text="Spectrune will be installed to the folder below. To install to a different drive or folder, edit the path directly:" />
        <Control Id="FolderEdit" Type="PathEdit" X="20" Y="90" Width="330" Height="18" Property="INSTALLFOLDER" />

        <Control Id="Back" Type="PushButton" X="156" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Text="Back" />
        <Control Id="Next" Type="PushButton" X="212" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Default="yes" Text="Next" />
        <Control Id="Cancel" Type="PushButton" X="304" Y="243" Width="56" Height="17" Cancel="yes" Text="Cancel">
          <Publish Event="SpawnDialog" Value="CancelDlg" />
        </Control>
      </Dialog>

      <!-- WelcomeDlg/VerifyReadyDlg/ExitDialog's Next/Back/Finish buttons
           have no built-in target for this (non-EULA, non-patch) flow —
           see the comment above the Fragment. -->
      <!-- Makes VerifyReadyDlg's bundled "Update"-labeled button show
           instead of "Install" when this run is actually a major upgrade
           over an already-installed older version — WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED
           is set by FindRelatedProducts, which always runs before
           WelcomeDlg is shown (see the standard InstallUISequence
           positions). Without this, VerifyReadyDlg's Install button
           (ShowCondition="NOT Installed AND ALLUSERS") always wins
           instead, since Installed reflects only this exact ProductCode
           (which regenerates every release, so it's never "installed"
           yet from its own point of view) — a fresh install and an
           in-place upgrade looked identical to the user before this. -->
      <Publish Dialog="WelcomeDlg" Control="Next" Property="WixUI_InstallMode" Value="Update" Condition="WIX_UPGRADE_DETECTED" Order="0" />
      <Publish Dialog="WelcomeDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="InstallDirDlg" Order="1">1</Publish>
      <Publish Dialog="InstallDirDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="WelcomeDlg" Order="1">1</Publish>
      <Publish Dialog="InstallDirDlg" Control="Next" Event="NewDialog" Value="VerifyReadyDlg" Order="1">1</Publish>
      <Publish Dialog="VerifyReadyDlg" Control="Back" Event="NewDialog" Value="InstallDirDlg" Order="1">1</Publish>
      <Publish Dialog="ExitDialog" Control="Finish" Event="EndDialog" Value="Return" Order="999" />
    </UI>
  </Fragment>
</Wix>
